WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.349 align:start position:0%
 
in silence  they grieved no one could doubt the  impact  matt ratner made on east grinstead rugby  club  in just three years as head coach  a whole other world to his role as a  police sergeant  he was such a key member of our  management team and our  our playing and coaching staff  i don't think we'll be able to replace  him there's no he was one in a million  and i know people say these things but  he really was  matt rachner oversaw the training of  players of all ages  including 500 children some as young as  four  who too fell silent tributes have been  paid here as officers who've lost their  lives in the line of duty  are remembered on national police  memorial day  sergeant ratner was the eighth officer  to be shot dead in the uk  in the last 20 years  metropolitan police commissioner dame  cressida dick  was among those who laid a wreath in  their memory the day was also marked  with a service  held online due to the pandemic which  included a tribute from the prince of  wales  the dreadful incident in croydon on  friday  is the latest heartbreaking evidence of  the risks  faced by our officers daily  as part of the memorial lizzie harper  the wife of pc andrew harper  who was killed last year lit a candle  now she's joined forces with another  police widow  to call for more support for those whose  loved ones have been killed while on  duty  i mean i sent leslie a letter or a card  very early on with my details in it  and if i was in a position to i'd do the  same with matt's family  i just think that the idea of having  somebody that's been there and done it  and fully understands you might just  help you through those really difficult  dark days  for these first team the loss of their  friend and coach  has been hard to comprehend there aren't  many middle-aged people  still trying to play competitive rugby  and my inspiration for that  came from from that he was a  he had a way of inspiring people the way  he was with players with  his little blocks of wood that he  pretended with players the  sketches on the bar and he had so much  inspiration  uh it's going to be it's going to be a  real hard time without him  the more that emerges about matt whether  as rugby coach or colleague in the  police  the more clear it becomes what an  extraordinary man he was  and how keenly he would be missed emma  birchley sky news east grinstead

